Subject: specify measurement error for non-linear model
Message-ID: <SYtfsZ6C7lw.ML42mOuOAAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/brms-users/c/SYtfsZ6C7lw/m/ML42mOuOAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I am analyzing a non-linear relationship where the predictor variable has some measurement error. I have specified the model as:

> form = Y ~ a + b * me(logX, log.sd <http://log.sd>)

> priors <- c(set_prior("normal(0,1)", nlpar = "a"),
+             set_prior("normal(0,1)", nlpar = "b"))

nl2 <- brm(bf(formula = form,
+              b ~ 1 + (1|G1), a ~ (1|G2), nl=T),
+            data = newdat2, prior = priors,
+            family = negbinomial(link="log"),
+            iter = 1000, chains = 4, cores = 4,
+            control = list(adapt_delta = 0.9),
+            save_model = "brm-mod-NB-ME-nl.txt")

This model does not compile - when I run the brm() call, it just moves to the cursor at the next line.

I'm not sure why this is happening. Is it not possible to specify measurement error in non-linear models in brms? Any help will be much appreciated.

Thanks,
Meghna

From u6e3dd74b422c061b at groups.google.com  Wed Dec 13 19:42:17 2017
From: u6e3dd74b422c061b at groups.google.com (Paul Buerkner)
Date: Wed, 13 Dec 2017 19:42:17 +0000
Subject: Re: [brms-users] specify measurement error for non-linear model
Message-ID: <SYtfsZ6C7lw.neQqR1-SAAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <SYtfsZ6C7lw.ML42mOuOAAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
References: <SYtfsZ6C7lw.ML42mOuOAAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/brms-users/c/SYtfsZ6C7lw/m/neQqR1-SAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It is possible, but you cannot specify me() in the non-linear formula itself, since this formula is literally translated into stan code and me(...) doesn't make sense to Stan.

Instead, you can merge "me()" into b, which results in:

bf(formula = Y ~ a + b,
   b ~ 0 + me(logX, log.sd <http://log.sd>) + (0 + me(logX, log.sd <http://log.sd>) | G1), a ~ (1|G2), nl=T)

In your special case, you might even further simplify your model to not using non-linear syntax at all:

formula = Y ~ me(logX, log.sd <http://log.sd>) + (me(logX, log.sd <http://log.sd>) | G1)

